      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{5ecb54c50739a41ff0269047|full_name}: \nSDK3 will be supported for the foreseeable future and beyond-- it is our current mainline content creation pipeline. \nOf course, if you're asking about what we'll support 20 years from now or some such-- correct, I don't have an answer for you.  VRChat does not ever guarantee an infinite (or *any* length) compatibility lifespan for any given content. We can give you best advice and tell you our plans as soon as they're solidified, which we have done.\nI will repeat myself: SDK2 is deprecated, will not receive new features, and will not receive priority for bug fixes (unless they're critical, game-breaking failures). It is in maintenance mode. We **strongly** recommend that all creators move to SDK3 as soon as reasonable-- if not for old projects, then definitely for new ones. \nThat being said, the bug reported in the OP is not a SDK2 bug. It is a Unity 2017 to 2019 Android bug. The shaders breaking is due to a Unity issue with older asset bundles, **not a change we've made.** We're investigating the cause, regardless.\n***The bug you are experiencing can be fixed by upgrading your world project from 2017 to 2018 then 2019, then reuploading.*** You can still use SDK2 for these projects, although I would again encourage you to swap to SDK3-- or at least plan to use SDK3 for your new projects.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
